  "prediction": "**The Holman Rule: A Summary of Congressional History and Precedents**\n\nThe Holman Rule, a congressional rule established in 1835, separates policy questions from funding decisions by prohibiting the inclusion of legislative provisions in appropriation bills unless they meet specific criteria. The rule, named after Representative William Holman of Indiana, has undergone several changes and interpretations over the years, with the most recent revision in 2017.\n\n**History of the Rule**\n\nThe Holman Rule originated in 1835 as a response to the increasing problem of delay in enacting appropriations due to the inclusion of non-essential legislative provisions. The rule was formalized in 1838 and has undergone several revisions, including a significant change in 1876 that expanded the rule to allow for amendments that reduce expenditures.\n\n**Current Form of the Rule**\n\nThe current version of the Holman Rule, adopted in 2017, states that a provision changing existing law may not be reported in a general appropriation bill, except for provisions that retrench expenditures by reducing amounts of money in the bill. The rule also allows for amendments that reduce the number of officers or the salaries of federal employees.\n\n**Precedents and Interpretations**\n\nThe House has interpreted the Holman Rule through precedents that have incrementally narrowed its application. The rule does not allow for retrenchments that are speculative or contingent on an event. Additionally, the rule does not apply to funds other than those appropriated in the pending general appropriations bill.\n\n**Recent Developments**\n\nIn 2017, the House adopted a separate order that reinstated language from the pre-1983 version of the rule, allowing for retrenchments that reduce the number of officers or the salaries of federal employees. This change opened the door to the consideration of additional amendments, including one that would have reduced the salary of the Administrator of the Western Area Power Administration to $1.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe Holman Rule remains an important part of congressional procedure, separating policy questions from funding decisions. While the rule has undergone several changes and interpretations over the years, its core purpose remains the same: to prevent the inclusion of non-essential legislative provisions in appropriation bills.",
